# Docker Tutorial Markdown

- Write for junior developers learning Docker. Teach Docker concepts first; use Java, Python, and Rust as examples, not as the main lesson.
- Keep `README.md` as the landing page and `docker-mastery-multitrack/docker-curriculum-guide.md` as the canonical module overview.
- Preserve the `00-11` learning path. When changing module names, links, or estimates, update related docs in the same change.
- Keep student-facing tone practical and calm. Avoid hype, victory-lap maintainer notes, internal AI artifacts, and unexplained placeholders.
- Prefer tested copy-paste commands. If a snippet is conceptual or not runnable from its folder, label it clearly.
- Use `/api/tasks` for the shared Task API unless a lesson explicitly explains proxying or path rewriting.
- Keep license, clone URL, prerequisites, ports, and time estimates consistent across README, curriculum guide, and learning objectives.
- Mark local-only credentials, classroom shortcuts, and dev-only assumptions where students will see them.
